Implement the next incremental version of the existing Log Streamer application.

IMPORTANT:
Do not redesign or replace the existing architecture.
Keep the current FastAPI backend, WebSocket implementation, NAS-mounted log source, dynamic log discovery, search/regex filtering, rule-based error detection, and existing Kubernetes connectivity.

This is an incremental UI + backend improvement.

==================================================
1. UI PRIORITY: MAXIMIZE LOG VIEW
==================================================

Rename the page title:

"Operations Log Streamer"
→
"Log Streamer"

Remove unnecessary permanent UI elements that consume vertical or horizontal space.

The log viewport must receive the maximum possible available width and height.

Remove the permanent display of:
- number of current log files
- archive status/details
- unnecessary informational text
- permanent live connection banners

The log viewer should occupy the majority of the screen.

==================================================
2. CONFIGURATION / JOB SPEC
==================================================

Do not hardcode the namespace in .env.

The namespace must come from the job specification YAML.

The job spec will define the application/environment-specific values.

Keep secrets and infrastructure configuration in .env, but do not use .env as the source for the target namespace.

The namespace from the job spec must be used for Kubernetes discovery and log discovery.

==================================================
3. STATIC LOGS SHOULD BE THE DEFAULT
==================================================

Do NOT start a continuous live WebSocket stream automatically when the page loads.

Default behavior:

1. Discover the relevant pods/log files.
2. Load the latest available log entries.
3. Display them as static historical data.
4. Do not continuously tail/send new log entries unless the user explicitly starts live mode.

Live streaming must be an explicit user action.

Use a clear action such as:

"Start"

When live mode is active, change the action to:

"Stop"

==================================================
4. LIVE STREAMING
==================================================

When the user clicks Start:

- establish the WebSocket connection
- begin tailing the relevant current log files
- send new log entries to the browser
- show a small temporary "Live stream connected" notification
- notification should automatically disappear after approximately 2 seconds

Do not keep the connection banner permanently occupying log-view space.

When Stop is clicked:

- stop the live stream
- close/clean up the WebSocket session
- stop file tailing
- release session resources

Live streaming must use bounded buffers and backpressure.

Do not allow an unbounded in-memory log buffer.

If the user pauses a live stream, do not destroy the session unnecessarily.
Pause should stop automatic UI advancement while keeping the stream state manageable.

When resumed, continue from the correct position.

==================================================
5. LOG ROW LAYOUT
==================================================

Current layout:

pod | timestamp | level | message

Change it to:

┌──────────────────┬────────┬───────────────────────────────┐
│ pod-name         │ level  │ log message                  │
│ timestamp        │        │                               │
└──────────────────┴────────┴───────────────────────────────┘

The pod name and timestamp must be vertically stacked in the same column.

Example:

pega-web-8abc123
2026-09-17 03:19:01.353

Then:

INFO    actual log message...

This saves horizontal space and gives the log message substantially more width.

Keep level styling and existing error/warning/info detection.

==================================================
6. FILTER PANEL
==================================================

Do not display the complete filter controls permanently.

At the right side of the primary configuration bar, add a filter icon.

Clicking the filter icon should expand a filter panel below the configuration bar using a smooth animation.

Clicking it again should collapse the panel.

The filter panel should contain the existing filter controls, including:

- search
- regex
- log level
- scope
- pod
- other existing filters

Do not remove existing filtering functionality.

==================================================
7. CHANGE "OPEN STREAM"
==================================================

Replace "Open stream" with:

"Start"

When live mode is active, use:

"Stop"

The terminology should clearly communicate that Start initiates the streaming session.

==================================================
8. LOG HISTORY PAGINATION
==================================================

The current implementation only displays 100 entries.

Do not treat 100 entries as the maximum available history.

100 entries should be the initial batch size.

Initial behavior:

- load the latest 100 entries
- position the viewport at the latest entry at the bottom

Ordering must be:

older logs
    ↓
newer logs
    ↓
latest log at bottom

When the user scrolls upward and approaches the beginning of the currently loaded history:

- automatically request the previous 100 entries
- show a loading indicator
- prepend the new entries to the existing history
- preserve the user's current visual scroll position
- do not jump the viewport
- allow the user to continue scrolling upward

Continue this process until no older logs are available.

Do not use simple page-number pagination.

Use cursor/position based pagination wherever possible.

The cursor should retain enough information to continue reading from the correct log file/offset/timestamp when multiple files or rotated logs are involved.

==================================================
9. NEWEST LOGS MUST REMAIN AT THE BOTTOM
==================================================

The log viewport must always follow this mental model:

TOP = oldest currently loaded logs
BOTTOM = newest logs

On initial load:

- automatically position the viewport at the bottom.

For live mode:

- if the user is already at the bottom and auto-scroll is enabled, new logs should automatically appear at the bottom.

If the user manually scrolls upward:

- do not force them back to the bottom
- automatically disable auto-scroll or otherwise prevent forced movement

Optionally show a small "new logs" indicator allowing the user to return to the latest position.

==================================================
10. AUTO-SCROLL
==================================================

Keep Auto-scroll.

Correct behavior:

AUTO-SCROLL ON:
- new live messages move the viewport to the latest log.

USER SCROLLS UP:
- stop forcing the viewport downward.

USER RETURNS TO BOTTOM:
- auto-scroll can resume.

Do not continuously scroll the user away from historical logs they are investigating.

==================================================
11. REFRESH / RELOAD BUTTON
==================================================

The existing reload icon should mean:

"Refresh current log data"

In static mode:
- re-run discovery
- reload the latest available logs
- preserve the current filters where possible

In live mode:
- the continuous stream is already updating the data, so the refresh control can be hidden or disabled if appropriate.

Do not interpret this as a browser page reload.

==================================================
12. ALL PODS
==================================================

Keep the existing ALL PODS capability.

When ALL PODS is selected, do not depend on a fixed pod name.

First connect to Kubernetes using the existing cluster session.

For the selected namespace/workload:

1. Query Kubernetes for currently active pods.
2. Determine the current active pod(s).
3. Determine the relevant container(s).
4. Discover their corresponding log files on the NAS.
5. Load their logs.

If there is one replica:
- load that pod.

If there are multiple replicas:
- load all relevant active pods.

The user should not have to manually select changing pod names just because Kubernetes restarted a pod.

==================================================
13. POD RESTART / CONTINUITY
==================================================

This is a critical requirement.

Pod names are ephemeral.

Do not use pod name as the identity of the logical log stream.

Use a stable workload identity such as:

namespace + workload/deployment + container

Kubernetes should be used to identify current active pods.

The NAS log files should be used to reconstruct historical continuity.

Do NOT rely exclusively on Kubernetes Pod creationTimestamp to determine historical log continuity.

Creation timestamps are useful metadata, but Kubernetes may no longer contain terminated/deleted Pod objects.

The actual log history on NAS is the authoritative source for historical continuity.

==================================================
14. CONTINUOUS LOG HISTORY ACROSS POD RESTARTS
==================================================

Example:

Pod A:
created 01:00
logs from 01:00 to 02:30

Pod B:
created 02:31
logs from 02:31 to current time

The user should see:

01:00 Pod A
01:01 Pod A
...
02:30 Pod A
02:31 Pod B
02:32 Pod B
...
current Pod B

as one continuous chronological log stream.

The user should NOT need to manually switch between Pod A and Pod B.

When older history is requested, the backend should automatically locate previous pod log files/archive files where available.

==================================================
15. TIMESTAMP ORDERING
==================================================

For ALL PODS, merge log entries chronologically using the timestamp contained in the actual log event.

Primary ordering:
- log event timestamp

Secondary ordering where timestamps are identical:
- file timestamp / file offset / deterministic source ordering

Pod creation timestamp should be used for discovery and continuity inference, not as the primary ordering mechanism.

Do not simply concatenate pod logs in arbitrary pod-name order.

==================================================
16. LOG FILE ROTATION / ARCHIVES
==================================================

Preserve the existing archive handling.

Do not load entire archives into memory.

Read archives incrementally and only retrieve the required ranges.

Do not modify files on the NAS.

If local temporary extraction/copying is already part of the existing implementation, preserve that behavior and clean up temporary resources appropriately.

==================================================
17. BROWSER PERFORMANCE
==================================================

Do not allow the browser DOM to grow indefinitely as more historical logs are loaded.

Use virtualized/windowed rendering or an equivalent bounded rendering strategy where appropriate.

The backend may have access to a large amount of historical data, but the browser should only render the rows necessary for the visible viewport plus a reasonable buffer.

This is especially important because the user can scroll through thousands or potentially millions of log entries.

==================================================
18. SESSION / RESOURCE MANAGEMENT
==================================================

Each user streaming session must have bounded resources.

Ensure:

- WebSocket cleanup
- file reader cleanup
- temporary resource cleanup
- bounded buffers
- backpressure
- stale session cleanup
- no unbounded accumulation of log messages
- multiple users/sessions remain isolated

Do not create a new expensive Kubernetes/NAS connection for every log row.

Reuse existing connections/readers where appropriate.

==================================================
19. RESPONSIVE LOG VIEW
==================================================

The log viewport should dynamically consume all remaining available screen height.

When:
- filter panel is collapsed
- connection notification disappears
- unnecessary status information is hidden

the log viewport should expand automatically.

When:
- filter panel expands

the log viewport should shrink accordingly rather than creating an unnecessary second scroll area.

Avoid nested scrollbars wherever possible.

==================================================
